  "body": {
    "id": "thm:unequal-three-arm-effective-certificate",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every admissible \\(n\\), use only the schedules \\[M_n=\\lfloor n^{1/72}\\rfloor\\vee1,\\quad T_n=2^{\\lfloor(\\log_2n)/12\\rfloor},\\quad\\varepsilon_n=T_n^{-1},\\quad G_n=\\lceil n^{3/4}\\rceil,\\quad\\delta_n=n^{-3/2}.\\] Exact algebraic arithmetic in \\(\\mathbb Q(\\alpha_n)\\) computes \\(B_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n}^\\dagger\\); the evaluator returns rational \\(U_{n,G_n}^\\dagger\\), \\(B^-_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n,\\delta_n}\\), and \\(B^+_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n,\\delta_n}\\) satisfying \\[B^-_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n,\\delta_n}\\le B_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n}^\\dagger\\le B^+_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n,\\delta_n},\\qquad B^+_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n,\\delta_n}-B^-_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n,\\delta_n}\\le\\delta_n,\\] and \\[n^{4/3}\\{n^{-1}-U_{n,G_n}^\\dagger\\}\\le n^{4/3}\\{n^{-1}-R_n(c^\\dagger)\\}\\le n^{4/3}\\{n^{-1}-B^-_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n,\\delta_n}\\}.\\] The distance of each displayed endpoint from \\(\\mathfrak L^\\dagger\\) is at most \\[\\beta^\\dagger(\\varepsilon_n,T_n)+C_{\\varepsilon_n,T_n}^\\dagger(1+M_n)^6n^{-1/6}+\\frac{n^{4/3}}{4G_n^2}+\\Delta_{\\mathrm{shell}}^\\dagger(n,M_n)+n^{4/3}\\{B^+_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n,\\delta_n}-B^-_{n,\\varepsilon_n,T_n,\\delta_n}\\}.\\] Every term in this single-schedule budget converges to zero, including \\(\\Delta_{\\mathrm{shell}}^\\dagger(n,M_n)\\to0\\), \\(\\beta^\\dagger(\\varepsilon_n,T_n)\\to0\\), and the scaled algebraic enclosure term. For every rational \\(\\eta>0\\), \\(\\mathsf E\\) terminates with a computable \\(N^\\dagger(\\eta)\\) such that the displayed error sum is at most \\(\\eta\\) for every \\(n\\ge N^\\dagger(\\eta)\\); isolating intervals are refined within the width-\\(\\delta_n\\) request until the enclosure term meets its assigned rational tolerance. This is a cross-sample modulus rather than a same-sample action-grid certificate.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "T",
      "\\varepsilon",
      "\\delta",
      "\\alpha_n",
      "B^-_{n,\\varepsilon,T,\\delta}",
      "B^+_{n,\\varepsilon,T,\\delta}",
      "B_{n,\\varepsilon,T}^\\dagger",
      "R_n(c)",
      "c^\\dagger"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:risk-game",
      "lem:permutation-orbit-bayes-lower",
      "thm:algebraic-certificate-polynomial-complexity"
    ],
    "justification": "The explicit error budget turns a limiting theorem into a finite, checkable claim and separates cross-sample convergence from action-grid discretization.",
    "gap": "The accepted parent bounds the two certificates at a selected \\(n\\) by \\(1/(4G^2)\\) but gives no computable threshold or allocation-shell modulus linking different population sizes.",
    "consumer": "A protocol designer can certify worst-case precision at a planned sample size from one prespecified sequence of tube widths, truncations, allocation radii, action grids, and rational enclosure widths.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix inputs satisfying the displayed positivity condition.  The established algebraic-certificate theorem applies with exactly \\((n,T,\\varepsilon,\\delta)\\) and returns rational endpoints \\(B^-_{n,\\varepsilon,T,\\delta}\\) and \\(B^+_{n,\\varepsilon,T,\\delta}\\) after a number of bit operations polynomial in the displayed input length.  It proves\n\\[\n B^-_{n,\\varepsilon,T,\\delta}\n \\le B_{n,\\varepsilon,T}^\\dagger\n \\le B^+_{n,\\varepsilon,T,\\delta},\n \\qquad\n B^+_{n,\\varepsilon,T,\\delta}-B^-_{n,\\varepsilon,T,\\delta}\n \\le\\delta.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\nFor any outcome-independent design and estimator, its maximum risk over schedules is at least its Bayes risk under the genuine tube prior.  The permutation-orbit Bayes lemma bounds that Bayes risk below by \\(B_{n,\\varepsilon,T}^\\dagger\\).  Taking the infimum over designs and estimators in the risk-game definition gives\n\\[\n R_n(c^\\dagger)\\ge B_{n,\\varepsilon,T}^\\dagger\n \\ge B^-_{n,\\varepsilon,T,\\delta}.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\nSubtracting (17) from \\(n^{-1}\\) and multiplying by the positive number \\(n^{4/3}\\) gives the final finite-sample deficit certificate.  Termination and the width claim come from the algebraic-certificate theorem; no cross-sample convergence assertion is used.",
